---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CriticalityDivider from '../components/CriticalityDivider.astro';
import interferometer from '../assets/research/interferometer.png';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const research = (await getCollection('projects'))
  .filter((p) => p.data.featured)
  .sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);

const publications = [
  {
    authors: 'B. A. Kılıç, Ö. B. Akan',
    title: 'Neural-Inspired Multi-Agent Molecular Communication Networks for Collective Intelligence',
    venue: 'MolCom 2026 — 10th Workshop on Molecular Communications',
    href: base + '/pdfs/projects/molcom/MOLCOM26_paper.pdf',
  },
];
---

<Base title="Research" description="Boran Kılıç's research — optical quantum information, quantum sensing with NV centers, and criticality in complex systems.">
  <header class="page-head shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
    <h1>What I work on</h1>
    <div class="page-intro">
      <p>
        My main interest is <strong>optical quantum information</strong> — how
        information is carried by light and by the matter it couples to, and how we
        read it out cleanly enough to be useful. Most of my hands-on work has been
        experimental: building and characterising the optical, microwave, and
        electronic pieces that turn a delicate quantum signal into a measurement you
        can trust.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alongside that, I keep returning to a question in complex systems: how
        rich, collective behaviour emerges from many simple interacting parts, and
        why so many of these systems sit near a critical point. I'm still learning,
        and I try to let the physics — not the hype — set the pace.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="shell"><CriticalityDivider label="optical quantum information" /></div>

  <section class="shell focus">
    <h2>Cavity phase readout for an entanglement-assisted telescope</h2>
    <p>
      In the Lukin group I work on the <strong>G12 phasecheck interferometer</strong>,
      the single-node phase-readout subsystem of an entanglement-assisted optical
      telescope. It is a fibre Mach–Zehnder interferometer built around the reflection
      arm of a silicon-vacancy (SiV) nanophotonic cavity. Its job is to measure the
      <em>frequency-dependent phase</em> of the cavity's reflection coefficient across
      the SiV optical transition — the quantity that tells you whether the cavity is
      over- or under-coupled, which the reflected intensity alone cannot.
    </p>
    <figure class="fig">
      <Image src={interferometer} alt="Blueprint of the G12 phasecheck interferometer: laser preparation, in-fibre and free-space AOMs, SiV cavity, 46 m fibre delay spool, and APD readout." widths={[600, 1000, 1339]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
      <figcaption>
        The G12 phasecheck setup — two lasers (a fixed Ti:Sa reference and a tunable
        Toptica probe) interleaved through AOM gates, an unbalanced fibre MZI with the
        SiV cavity in one arm and a 46 m delay spool in the other, and an FPGA that
        phase-locks the fringe through the on-table AOMs.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      My part of it is the practical end: understanding what every component
      contributes, deriving the APD signal, path-matching the two arms so the fringe
      stays visible, and working through the AOM-based phase-locking that holds the
      interferometer at a chosen operating point. I wrote up the full walkthrough as
      an internal note.
    </p>
    <p class="focus-links mono">
      <a href={base + '/pdfs/research/g12_interferometry_theory.pdf'} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Setup &amp; locking note (PDF) ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="shell"><CriticalityDivider label="quantum sensing" /></div>

  <section class="shell focus">
    <h2>NV-center magnetometry at TÜBİTAK Quantum Technologies</h2>
    <p>
      At the <strong>Quantum Technologies department of TÜBİTAK BİLGEM</strong> I worked
      on magnetometry with nitrogen-vacancy (NV) centers in diamond — atomic defects
      whose fluorescence tracks the local magnetic field, giving a sensitive,
      room-temperature magnetometer. I helped build and characterise the optical and
      microwave setup: resolving the hyperfine structure of the optically detected
      magnetic resonance (ODMR) spectrum, setting up lock-in detection on a
      frequency-modulated microwave drive, and measuring the sensitivity and dynamic
      range of the instrument. I also wrote the data-processing and machine-learning
      pipeline for extracting field estimates from noisy ODMR sweeps.
    </p>
    <p class="focus-links mono">
      <a href={base + '/projects/nv-center-magnetometry'}>Read the full write-up →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="shell"><CriticalityDivider label="selected projects" /></div>

  <section class="shell threads">
    {research.map((p) => (
      <a class="thread" href={`${base}/projects/${p.id}`}>
        <span class="thread-year mono">{p.data.year}</span>
        <span class="thread-body">
          <span class="thread-title">{p.data.title}</span>
          <span class="thread-blurb">{p.data.blurb}</span>
          <span class="thread-more mono">Read more →</span>
        </span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </section>

  <div class="shell"><CriticalityDivider label="publications" /></div>

  <section class="shell pubs">
    <ol>
      {publications.map((pub) => (
        <li>
          <p class="pub-title">
            <a href={pub.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{pub.title}</a>
          </p>
          <p class="pub-meta">{pub.authors}</p>
          <p class="pub-venue mono">{pub.venue}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="scholar mono">
      Full list on <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=9SPQHJEAAAAJ&hl=en" target="_blank" rel="me noopener">Google Scholar ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-head { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .page-head h1 { margin: 0.15em 0 0.5em; }
  .page-intro { max-width: 40rem; }
  .page-intro p { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 1.1rem; }

  .focus { max-width: 42rem; }
  .focus h2 { font-size: var(--step-2); margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .focus p { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .focus-links { margin-top: 0.4rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .fig { margin: 1.8rem 0; }
  .fig :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--faint);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: #fff;
  }
  .fig figcaption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .threads { display: grid; gap: 0.4rem; }
  .thread {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 4.5rem 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 1.3rem 0.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .thread:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--faint); }
  .thread:hover { text-decoration: none; background: var(--accent-wash); }
  .thread-year { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .thread-title {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 460;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .thread:hover .thread-title { color: var(--accent-deep); }
  .thread-blurb { display: block; color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .thread-more { font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--accent); }

  .pubs ol { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .pubs li { padding: 0.5rem 0 1.2rem; }
  .pub-title { margin: 0 0 0.2rem; font-size: 1.12rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .pub-meta { margin: 0 0 0.15rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .pub-venue { margin: 0; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .scholar { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 0.5rem; }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .thread { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.3rem; }
  }
</style>
